00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:48.920
So maybe like a big bit of a backstory and kind of how we ended up here. So around 2019,

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:55.640
the Arturia function launched an initiative called Atelier with the goal to upscale the

00:04:55.640 --> 00:05:00.679
Arturial function. So basically in data science, programming, and communication.

00:05:01.399 --> 00:05:07.239
And this has had a huge, huge impact and has been a huge success and is sponsored by our group,

00:05:07.239 --> 00:05:14.920
Chief Actuary. And it's now not only just an Arturial function, but has an impact on

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:20.920
in the whole organization. So that means that we have all these analytical capabilities,

00:05:21.559 --> 00:05:27.640
but the question is now how do we share these capabilities among our colleagues? So including

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:31.239
those who code and those who do not code. And if somebody asks, well, if you code, then you should

00:05:31.239 --> 00:05:36.679
not have a problem. You can use the code easily, but you have a problem where let's say there's a

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:40.519
really great R exclusive package, but you basically work with Python.

00:05:41.239 --> 00:05:45.320
And there, this spec is no longer available to you. So you can then agree with your colleagues,

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:48.839
so let's keep it simple. Let's all just code in R. We have everybody code in the same language,

00:05:48.839 --> 00:05:53.320
so we all can understand each other. So your colleague writes a script that can help you to

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:58.839
do your work. And you open the script and you realize you can't make any sense of it. You don't

00:05:58.839 --> 00:06:03.880
know which functions to run first, what those arguments mean. You don't know if a variable is

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:09.079
a data frame, a list, or a vector. So then either you spend a great deal trying to understand

00:06:09.079 --> 00:06:16.279
somebody else's code or you just give up and you rewrite your own script. So then you have a risk

00:06:16.279 --> 00:06:22.679
that you're not only duplicating efforts across teams, but sometimes you also end up duplicating

00:06:22.679 --> 00:06:28.839
efforts within the same team. So we end up basically splitting the expertise instead

00:06:28.839 --> 00:06:36.519
of leveraging it. So this is kind of the main problem that I've seen. And this solution is a

00:06:36.519 --> 00:06:43.399
way to kind of counter it. And that's why the way to go is to centralize your logic into an API.

00:06:43.399 --> 00:06:48.279
And that means you can have R and SAS, for example, calling an endpoint written in Python.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:54.200
So basically, later you can build a shiny app that calls that exactly same endpoint without

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:59.559
having to duplicate that effort. So the idea is to bring the logic of your code where the user is

00:06:59.559 --> 00:07:10.440
already comfortable. And the place where most users are comfortable, whether we like it or not,

00:07:11.079 --> 00:07:17.079
is Excel. So Excel can make HTTP requests. And therefore, you can basically,

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:23.640
you're no longer limited to the native capabilities of Excel. And it's installed

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:29.399
in every machine. Every person, whether they like it or not, is opening Excel at least once a day.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:37.559
And basically, you can access exactly the same capabilities that you could do

00:07:37.559 --> 00:07:44.600
with any other application that can make an HTTP request. So that's kind of basically the idea.

00:07:45.239 --> 00:07:49.559
And then if it becomes really successful, then you can then build a shiny app on that,

00:07:49.559 --> 00:07:54.040
because now you have driven adoption, people are using it. You can then build a shiny app

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:58.920
calling the exact same endpoint without having to duplicate the code. If you want to update,

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:05.399
you can update the API and any client is updated automatically. So you maintain only one version

00:08:05.399 --> 00:08:11.000
control, one endpoint, easy to maintain and easier to share across teams.

