Yeah, so I think there are two kind of important aspects. So one of this is that

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:04.440
Excel does not replace your coding preference. So if something is in an endpoint, and you want

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:11.159
to code in R, you can call that endpoint from R itself. So nothing stops you from continuing your

00:09:11.159 --> 00:09:16.440
preferred workflow. And maybe somebody developed something in Python, that now it has deployed

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:21.960
logic to an endpoint, and you can just continue your preferred method of working. So it's not

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:28.919
taking anything away. On the other hand, if you have your own code, and you know that

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:34.280
most people are not going to use your scripts, you're also less willing to share. But if you

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:39.080
realize that, well, if I share these scripts, many people are going to use it, then you're also

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:44.359
more willing to invest in your script, make it clearer, and be willing to share your work with

00:09:44.359 --> 00:09:52.200
others. So I think like if you prefer to code, the preference is that both that more and the

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:57.640
more people use your code, the bigger the impact you have within the company. So I don't see that

00:09:58.119 --> 00:10:03.880
there's a conflict in that people prefer to code and don't prefer to code.

00:10:04.599 --> 00:10:08.440
Awesome. Thank you for the clarification. I wanted to make sure that we all knew we were

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:14.440
talking about increasing accessibility in both directions, instead of making that smaller.

